# Chapter 15: any Terraform: what runs on this machine, what does not, and why
#
# Deliberator will be pointed at a company's real Terraform: other providers (Datadog, Zscaler, Vault), AWS services the emulator
# does not have, `data` lookups of things that only exist in the real account, variables without defaults, modules in git
# repositories that cannot be fetched, data sources that never answer. None of it runs cleanly, and none of it may crash the tool.
# `terraform.survey` finds every root under the directories it is given, reads its text, then makes REAL Terraform say it: init, plan
# (with the override file `terraform.local` writes), the dependency graph, and a plan of exactly the resources that can run. One row per
# root: a verdict (runs / runs-partly / does-not-run) and per problem a class (other-provider, unsupported-service, lookup, missing-var,
# module-fetch, other). The raw output of every Terraform call is kept first, in the survey's folder, as text.
# Then `terraform.local` with `supported-only` applies only what runs and lists every skipped resource with its class.
# The fixtures are the repository's own (core/test/fixtures/tf-survey), one root per class, copied so nothing is written into them.
# In the IDE: the same operations are in the Operations form (terraform.survey, terraform.local).
# @env local

### shell paths
# @cwd ..
# @capture root = out
pwd | tr -d '\n'

### shell copy-the-fixtures
# A folder of "repositories" (one root per class, one module they share, one library module nobody calls).
# @capture copy = out
d="{{root}}/.scratch/tour15"
rm -rf "$d" && mkdir -p "$d"
cp -r "{{root}}/core/test/fixtures/tf-survey/." "$d/"
git init -q "$d"
printf %s "$d"

### op terraform.survey survey
# Nine roots in the folder (the module `queue` is a module of `with-module`, `unused-library` is a library nobody calls: both listed,
# neither run). The `hang` root has a data source that sleeps for ten minutes: every Terraform run has 60 s here, then its process
# tree is killed and the data source it was waiting for is named.
# @timeout 15m
# @capture folder = $.folder
# @assert $.summary.runs == 2
# @assert $.summary.runs-partly == 5
# @assert $.summary.does-not-run == 2
# @assert $.roots[0].verdict == does-not-run
# @assert $.roots[1].verdict == does-not-run
# @assert $.roots[2].verdict == runs-partly
# @assert $.roots[3].verdict == runs-partly
# @assert $.roots[3].skipped[0].via == data.aws_vpc.main
# @assert $.roots[4].verdict == runs-partly
# @assert $.roots[5].verdict == runs-partly
# @assert $.roots[6].verdict == runs
# @assert $.roots[7].verdict == runs-partly
# @assert $.roots[8].verdict == runs
# @assert $.table contains unsupported-service aws_xray_group
# @assert $.table contains other-provider datadog
# @assert $.table contains missing-var environment
# @assert $.table contains module-fetch
# @assert $.table contains lookup data.external.slow
{"dirs": ["{{copy}}"], "budget-s": 60}

### shell the-table
# The table is a text file in the survey's folder, with the raw output of every Terraform call beside it (init.jsonl, graph.dot, plan-*.jsonl).
# @assert out contains VERDICT
# @assert out contains lookup data.aws_vpc.main
cat "{{folder}}/survey.txt" | head -40

### op terraform.survey again
# Asking for the latest answers at once from the persisted copy, with its age, and runs nothing.
# @assert $.table contains VERDICT
{"latest": true}

### op terraform.local apply-what-runs
# `supported-only`: the root `unsupported-type` has a queue and an X-Ray group; Floci has no X-Ray. Only the queue is applied
# (`-target`), the group is listed as skipped with its class.
# @timeout 8m
# @assert $.ok == true
# @assert $.summary contains Apply complete! Resources: 1 added
# @assert $.skipped[0].address == aws_xray_group.slow
# @assert $.skipped[0].class == unsupported-service
{"dir": "{{copy}}/roots/unsupported-type", "action": "apply", "supported-only": true, "budget-s": 120}

### aws sqs.ListQueues the-queue
# It really is on the emulator.
# @assert $.QueueUrls[0] contains svy-unsupported-plain
{"QueueNamePrefix": "svy-unsupported"}

### op terraform.local destroy-what-runs
# Destroy works the same way.
# @timeout 8m
# @assert $.ok == true
# @assert $.summary contains Destroy complete! Resources: 1 destroyed
{"dir": "{{copy}}/roots/unsupported-type", "action": "destroy", "supported-only": true, "budget-s": 120}

### shell clean-up
rm -rf "{{copy}}"
